<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">skin</ansi>

Take the hide, pelt, fur, scales or chitin off a carcass.

  <ansi fg="command">skin <corpse></ansi>

You need a knife: a skinning knife is best, but any one-handed dagger or
sword will do as a crude one. You must be able to see, you cannot do it in
combat, and the kill must be yours (or open to anyone). Skinning takes a few
rounds, longer for a big animal and shorter with a better knife.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What decides the result ━━━</ansi>

  Your <ansi fg="yellow">Dexterity</ansi> and <ansi fg="yellow">Perception</ansi> do most of the work, then your knife,
  then a little <ansi fg="yellow">salvage</ansi> skill. The result is graded crude, standard, fine,
  superb or pristine, and your knife caps the grade (<ansi fg="command">help tools</ansi>). Bigger,
  tougher animals are harder. A carcass that has lain a while gives one grade
  worse. A botched job tears the hide.

Skin <ansi fg="yellow">before</ansi> you butcher: butchering an unskinned carcass ruins the hide.
Raw hides rot in a few days; cure them at a tanning rack (<ansi fg="command">help cure-hide</ansi>)
or sell them to a tanner. See <ansi fg="command">help spoilage</ansi>.

